A||N||8||Bob Englert||Continuing Alignment Adventures||||11||
Z||000000||Bob Englert||12-27-2000||03:23 PM||||Sorry for the length of this, but I need to vent a little.  I do have a question at the end of my rant!<P>As I posted earlier, I have been having problems with the alignment after rebuilding the front suspension on one of my 87 GT's.  I have done this on 4 different Fieros, so I am pretty familiar with what is going on here.  From the way the car was driving I suspected a toe problem or caster problem.  The car had poor directional stability (caster?) and wanted to often pull strongly to the right (toe and/or caster).<P>The first response by the Goodyear store was to swap the 2 front tires.  Makes sense if their is a problem with the tires.  Problem was the same.<P>After asking on the Forum I printed out the alignment specs for the 87 and provided them when I went back to the store.  They did not provide a printout when the first did the alignment so I asked for one when they rechecked it.  The store manager/owner got real defensive when I asked for the printout and provided the specs and told me the printer wasn't working.  So and told him to write the values down and provide them.  He then said they would "do their best", I responded that I wanted it in spec unless there was a problem preventing it to be set properly.<P>I left the car with them and they called mid day and said that the caster on the right side was 1.4 deg while it should be 5 deg.  I had provided a GM caster kit (washers) to adjust if needed but these washers will only move it about 1 deg.  I asked if the 2 original washers could be put on the same side to move the upper control arm father to get it within spec but the service guy gave me some line about the threads moving and .....  Complete BS.  Said they would do it at my risk and that I would have to pay $55 to remove the control arm and another $55 for another alignment!  I told them to pull it down and I will go elsewhere.  <P>When I went to pick up the car I asked the owner if he would be willing to work with me or should I go elsewhere.  He proceeded to give me a lecture about not telling him how to do his job, etc.....   My point was that the 1.4 deg caster proves that the car was not aligned properly the first time, since nothing was said about the caster being out of spec.  They did produce a alignment printout and the front toe values were also out of spec on each side and the left rear toe was out.  They accused me of autocrossing it and knocking the rear toe out.  The manager told me to go elsewhere and I demanded ang did get a refund of the original alignment.  At least he did right in that case.<P>I will be taking the printout to another alignment shop and try to find a shop that is willing to do the job correctly.  I intend to move and replace the caster washers to get it closer to the correct spec before I take it back to another shop.  The values now are:<P>Front Settings:<BR>Camber:  L - 1.0 deg.       R - 0.2 deg.<BR>Caster:  L - 3.9 deg.       R - 1.4 deg.<BR>Toe:     L - -0.20 deg.     R - 0.46 deg.<P>Rear Settings:<BR>Camber:  L - -1.0 deg.      R - -1.0 deg.<BR>Toe:     L - 0.32 deg.      R - 0.08 deg.<P>Acording to the alignment print out, the right caster, both front toe values, and the left rear toe are out of specs.<P>I will need more adjustment than I can get from the GM washers.  Can I use properly sized steel washers in a stack that equal the thickness of the 2 GM wahsers to move the right control arm back to within specs (something like the MOOG caster kit does)?  With the spec at 5 deg, I think this is contributing to the wandering problem.<P>Sorry for the space, I feel a little better now.  I really think the store owner/manager was pissed that I knew what I was talking about and what I wanted done!<P>Happy New Year to all!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||11
Z||000001||bHooper||12-27-2000||05:55 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Bob, Mike wasn't kidding... when he said you were "a little hot, today".<P>I dropped my Jeep off this morning and asked them about my problem.  At the same time, I think I mentioned the pull in your car.  But that was b4 they worked on it.<P>Another alignment place mightbe the BP station on Cornwalis at Elm.  Charles Holland own's it, I have know him for 20 some years... a <B>real honest person</B>!  I think he has an alignment guy, you might want to call up there.  They also own the the BP at Muirs Chappel and Market ST.<P>Paul drove my car today, and felt the "wiggle".  He said it felt as if the car was trying to overcorrect itself.  I also ran it by Jack, at Goodyear... he didn't say a whole lot (wonder why?), but that I should have more of a understeer with the swaybar, instead of oversteer.  I am going to disconect the swaybar on friday and give it a try.<P>Best of luck with the blue car!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||12-27-2000||06:17 PM||||It was very frustrating when they reacted immediately when I asked to see the printout and were not interested in discussing how to solve the problem.<P>You are right, by the time I sat through the lecture I was hot.  I can't get away with treating my customers like that, I don't like it any better than anyone else when I am on the receiving end of it.<P>You probably shouldn't mention my name when you pick the Jeep up!<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||12-27-2000||07:32 PM||||Bob,<P>I know what your going through!<BR>I never thought of what you suggest but it sounds as if it will work if the physical properties of the control arm doesn't interfere with the correction.<BR>I had a shop tell me that there was no way to correct either caster OR camber on a Fiero and quickly proved them wrong by letting them put it on the lift! They looked, made comment and I took it someplace else! (they knew what was going on!)<BR>But on the other hand if all new parts are up front I don't understand why it's so far off specs!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bob Englert||12-27-2000||07:40 PM||||Could the way the new rubber control arm bushings were pressed into the arm significantly affect the caster?  I don't know if the rubber bushings are pressed to a mechanical stop (lip).<P>I am going to try to look for a set of thin stainless steel washers that I can use to load up the space behind the frame to move the control arm back.  Probably leave 1 washer in front to protect the bushing.<P>Also, the left side is withing specs but less than optimal.  The GM caster washers that I have should bring this side very close to correct.  For driavability, is it better to have both sides as close to the same as possible or should I move the left side to nominal value and try to get the right moved as far as possible?<P>Thanks to all.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-27-2000||10:09 PM||||It might just be the way I was brought up, and since most people in our town have dealerships service their vehicles, but I don't even consider taking my cars anywhere but a dealership. I take my Fiero to Pontiac, took my Sable to Ford, our Chryslers go to Chrysler, etc. <BR>After I installed poly on my Formula, I took it to my favorite Pontiac dealership/service center. Aside from the exceptional service I received, they aligned my Formula to spec within two hours, I was provided a printout, and I was on my way. Plus it was only $50 for 4/wheel alignment. Your case might be different since there is a "problem" according to Goodyear, but maybe Pontiac would have better luck?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||12-28-2000||08:20 AM||||My experiences with the service department of the local Pontiac dealer have been pretty poor.  When I had my Formula I rebuilt the front suspension with poly bushings.  I took the car into the dealership for an alignment and they could not get the steering wheel straight after 2 tries.  The wheel was about 30 deg off!  It was amazing!  Plus they charged $75 for a 4 wheel alignment.  They have told me that they didn't have any techs that were familiar with the Fiero anymore.  <P>I have had other work done at times and I felt that I was badly overcharged for the work they did.  <P>In Greensboro, the Pontiac dealer is not a very good service option for me.  <P>Bob<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||12-28-2000||08:41 AM||||You might try asking a better known body shop who they use for their alignments. If they can get a wrecked car aligned, a Fiero should not be too much trouble.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||12-28-2000||09:16 AM||||Bob, you may also want to check with the Ponitac Dealer in Kernersville.  They are friendly, at least.<P>hoop<p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 12-28-2000).]||||reg||1
